## Context

Analysis of the punctuality of 336,776 flights departing from New York in 2013 (nycflights13 dataset): identifying where delays are concentrated, by airline, season, and aircraft. The main challenge was to produce a reliable aggregated table without losing or double-counting flights.

## Data

336,776 flights and 16 airlines. The 2,512 flights without registration are kept in an explicit group rather than deleted, and scheduled, departed, and actually observed flights are distinguished to account for cancellations and missing values.

## Method

- Validated join (many-to-one), with a check on the number of flights before and after.
- Aggregation at the month × airline × aircraft × destination grain, with distinct counts to ensure each average relies on the correct denominator.
- Suite of automated checks, re-run on the exported file: key uniqueness, count consistency (delayed ≤ observed ≤ departed ≤ scheduled), reconciliation of totals with the source table.
- Delivery in an Excel dashboard.

## Results

- Marked seasonal differences between airlines: in January, an average delay of 24.2 min for ExpressJet compared to 3.8 min for Delta. Differences narrow in the summer, when all airlines deteriorate.
- For an equal volume of delays, two aircraft do not have the same reliability: relative to the number of departures, their delay rates range from 20.9% to 43.0%, which reverses the ranking.

## Lessons Learned and Limitations

An aggregation is only valuable if it is reconcilable with its source, and an average must be read alongside its sample size: SkyWest's average, calculated on only 32 flights, does not allow for any serious comparison.
